WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.240
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
On this episode,

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:02.550
we talked to doc.ai
about how they

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.925
use Federated Learning to
preserve privacy while training

00:00:04.925 --> 00:00:05.940
machine learning models.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.868
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.630
So going back to
your application,

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.440
you kind of mentioned that AI
was used for the patients that

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:15.100
are using it.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.670
Tell us a little bit more
about the actual app.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.530
AKSHAY SHARMA: Yeah, so we have
the medical research companion

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:21.460
app.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:25.420
And what it allows you
to do is basically be

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.080
able to collect some
important omics information.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.580
And we have reduced the friction
to be able to collect that.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.730
Like I mentioned earlier, you
can take a medical selfie which

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:36.400
is just a selfie, but it
extracts medical information

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.400
out of that.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.630
We can extract mood
and behavioral data out

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:40.720
of that selfie.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.630
But we can also take
pictures of your pill bottles

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.450
and understand what medication
and dosage and instructions

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:47.620
that exist on those.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.360
We can do that
pretty much for lab

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.360
results and medical
records very soon.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:55.160
We can also collect your
genetic information.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.400
So we don't do wet
sequencing, meaning,

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.470
that we don't get you sequenced.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:01.720
But we can collect
any of the raw data

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.310
that you have sequenced
yourself, for example,

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:08.050
from 23andMe or Ancestry, and be
able to bring that into the app

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:13.120
and keep it in the app and then
understand what's in the data.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.020
So one is it's all about
polyomics data collection

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.380
that the app allows you to do.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.200
But on the other
side, we basically

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.780
run what we call as
data trials, which

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.720
is the new version of what
medical research looks

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:31.090
like where we can actually
take users vertically,

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.680
based on their
disease or conditions,

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:37.360
and use the data that they've
collected so far to lead them

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.640
towards this research
with the right consent

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.000
and the right options so that
they can participate and get

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.230
some rewards.

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:46.330
The rewards could be
financial, but it will also

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.790
be more than that, like
insights and other predictions

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:50.480
that we can give on top of that.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.320
So largely, the
medical companion app

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:58.330
is a medical research app that
allows patients to understand

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:00.970
and see what are the newer ways
that they can find solutions

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:02.590
for their diseases today.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.090
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
How does the cloud

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:08.650
enable that collection and
then actual using of that data?

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.390
AKSHAY SHARMA: Right, so
we see that in many ways.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.840
So first of all,
for some something

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.140
like medical selfie, which
is basically a deep learning

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.540
model that we have
built, we have built it

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:22.600
on the cloud across
a lot of data

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.000
that we were able to
collect painstakingly

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.720
through different techniques.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.080
So the medical selfie
is a deep learning model

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.960
that can take an image
or a selfie of your face

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.310
and be able to predict
your sex, height, weight,

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:37.040
and age as parameters
out of that,

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.890
but then also creates further
predictions out of that.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:44.200
So now that's a model that
we built on the cloud--

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.440
on Google Cloud-- using a lot
of computational resources.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.720
Then we were able to
convert that model

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.670
to run on a mobile device.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:55.490
So we were able to take that
and made it do edge inferences.

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:57.760
So we can run it on a
phone, and it can actually

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.430
predict those variables
just sitting on the phone

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:04.360
without really revealing
or taking any of the images

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:09.090
that the patient or the member
is taking back to the server.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:13.090
So one thing that I see
there is that cloud enables

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:15.940
us to build models
like this that today

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.160
is much harder to build
just on the phone.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.890
But with newer approaches
like Federated Learning,

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.810
we can actually also train
and build models on the phone

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:28.510
across many, many, many users,
like hundreds of thousands

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:29.480
of users.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.330
So we may not need cloud
to even centralize the data

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:34.805
at some point in the future.

00:03:34.805 --> 00:03:37.180
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So what
exactly does Federated Learning

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:39.735
enable for the users who
are using the application?

00:03:39.735 --> 00:03:41.360
AKSHAY SHARMA: That's
a great question.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.690
So I think health
care data in general

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:46.540
is a very valuable asset.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:49.180
And I think it's highly
secure and private data

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:52.540
that every user
and every patient

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.440
should actually care for.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:57.700
So we think the future of where
all of the health information

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.520
will exist is only on
the edge, specifically

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.190
on the mobile phones
for the users.

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:07.150
And I think it's easy for
them to lose that information

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:10.360
and send it to a cloud if
they're not aware of how

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:11.630
that data is being used.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.020
So what we can do with
Federated Learning now

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:19.060
is provide guarantees for users
that it can stay on the device.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:22.600
But then we don't lose the
aspect of learning something

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.780
from the data, wherein
we can now build models

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:27.910
that can train on the device.

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:30.330
And we can build a better
model on the server

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:34.880
by just sort of sharing the
device-trained information,

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.670
which is usually like bits
of information, like numbers,

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.400
but not the real data itself.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:40.900
And imagine now
if we can do that

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.330
across a population of users
like hundreds of thousands

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:46.210
of users, then what we
get behind the scenes

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:49.300
is a much more robust model
that's much more diverse.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.150
And hopefully, it's much
more generalized than as

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:54.410
if it was trained
on a single user.

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.990
So that's what Federated
Learning can do.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:59.710
And then we can add layers
of security and tools

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.440
like differential privacy
where we don't even

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:05.110
learn anything about the
person that we are training on.

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:07.390
So in combination with
differential privacy

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:08.990
and Federated
Learning, I think we

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:11.300
have a very important
solution where

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:13.790
we can keep the data
on the edge and still

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:15.530
be able to learn
something from the data

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:19.193
without knowing who the user
is or what's in the data.

00:05:19.193 --> 00:05:21.860
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Stay tuned for
more conversations with doc.ai.

